Level 1: Basic Linux Commands
1. Create a directory called linux_practice in your home directory
   mkdir linux_practice
2. Inside it, create 5 empty files: file1.txt to file5.txt
   touch file1.txt
   touch file2.txt file3.txt file4.txt file5.txt
3. Write your name and today’s date into file1.txt
   vim file1.txt
4. Display the content of file1.txt using cat and less
   cat file1.txt
5. Copy file1.txt to backup_file1.txt
   cp file1.txt backup_file1.txt
6. Rename file2.txt to data.txt
   mv file2.txt data.txt
7. Delete file5.txt

Level 2: File Permissions & Ownership
8. Check permissions of all files using ls -l
9. Change permission of data.txt to rw-r-----
   chmod 640 data.txt
10. Make file3.txt executable
    chmod 755 file3.txt
11. Change ownership of file4.txt to another user (if available)
    chown ec2-user file4.txt
12. Explain the difference between r, w, x permissions
r - Read - 4 - Allows a user to view the contents of a file.
w - Write - 2 - Allows a user to modify, edit, or delete a file.
x - Execute - 1 - Allows a file to be run as a program or script.
Other  Group  Public


Level 3: Searching & Viewing Files
13. Search for the word Linux in all .txt files using grep
    grep "Linux" *.txt
14. Count number of lines, words, and characters in file1.txt
    wc file1.txt
15. Display last 10 lines of /var/log/messages or /var/log/syslog
    tail -10 /var/log/messages
16. Find all .txt files inside linux_practice
    find linux_practice -name "*.txt"

Level 4: Process & System Monitoring
17. List all running processes
    ps -ef
18. Find the PID of sshd or systemd
    ps -ef | grep sshd
19. Kill a process using kill command
    kill pid 1776
20. Check CPU and memory usage
    top
    lscpu
    free -h
21. Display disk usage using df and du
    df -h

Level 5: Package Management
22. Install tree package
    yum install tree -y
23. Remove tree package
    yum remove tree -y
24. Check installed version of git
    git --version
25. List all installed packages
    yum list installed

Level 6: Networking Basics
26. Check IP address of the system
    Hostname -I
27. Test connectivity to google.com
    ping google.com
28. Display open ports
    ss -tuln
29. Check DNS configuration
    cat /etc/resolv.conf
30. Download a file using wget or curl
    wget https://dl.k8s.io/release/v1.30.0/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl
